 ## Process-bound transition API
 
-`issue_initialization(policy) -> InitializationPermit` reauthenticates everything, captures the original process/hooks, and obtains real pre-import host and lease checks. It does not import or initialize JAX. One issuance attempt is allowed per process, including failure. An attempted reentry during capture/check makes the original attempt sticky-failed. The registry is installed before callback work, retaining original inputs on failure.
+`issue_initialization(policy) -> InitializationPermit` first retains the exact original `DevicePolicyAdmission` dictionary and its three original field objects (`phase`, immutable `blobs`, immutable `expected_pins`), then reauthenticates everything, captures the original process/hooks, and obtains real pre-import host and lease checks. It does not import or initialize JAX. One issuance attempt is allowed per process, including failure. An attempted reentry during capture/check makes the original attempt sticky-failed. The registry is installed before callback work, retaining original inputs on failure.
+
+The v2 local policy guard compares the original dictionary identity, exact field set and each field's identity before/after full reauthentication and around original hook calls. Replacement with equal bytes or an equal tuple also fails and remains sticky. Hooks receive the retained original phase and blobs rather than rereading mutable dataclass fields. Original input objects remain strongly held after rejection. This fixes the source-reviewed v1 local identity gap; it is not an actual authority-bypass claim. Nested phase-object integrity still uses full phase authentication and the selected process binding/snapshot obligations; this narrow guard does not newly claim a complete nested graph or arbitrary interpreter-global freeze.
 
 `initialize_once(permit) -> same permit` consumes that original permit before calling the initializer. It binds the genuine JAX module file/hash and independent spec, exact `jax.Device` type, original `memory_stats` class descriptor and bound method, original selection function/code identities, original device and PID/thread. It verifies the exact granted descriptor, x64 and retained default-device scope. This first implementation supports one globally visible/addressable local JAX TPU device and one process only. It does not multiply TensorCores/chips or repartition ciphertexts. A different subtype/topology needs an explicit reviewed extension, not a permissive fallback. No body scope is enabled by its return.
 
